  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely on cross-sectional self-reported survey data from a single city, which limits causal inference regarding green space visitation and health outcomes.",
      "what": "A cross-sectional survey of 718 adult residents in Chania, Crete, conducted from June to August 2024, evaluated the relationship between green space visitation, demographics, and physical health conditions including BMI, coronary artery disease, hypertension, and hypercholesterolemia. Logistic regression models revealed that living more than a 20-minute walk from green space increased the odds of hypertension (OR = 1.603), while visits lasting longer than one hour were linked to lower odds of coronary artery disease, hypertension, and hypercholesterolemia alongside increased physical activity duration.",
      "why": "The study demonstrates that visit duration and specific green space types correlate with metabolic and cardiovascular health outcomes independently of simple spatial proximity in a Mediterranean urban environment."
    }
  },
